All trades are going to essentially become highly trained and specialized QA/Architect types. This is already starting to happen in software, and it is going to spread everywhere over the next few years. SWEs are spending most of their time driving architectural tweaks in AI code, then manually testing those changes to cover edge cases. Some fields will take longer than others.

Engineers, Lawyers, Doctors, Finance/Accounting, Sales/Marketing, Plumbers/Electricians. Machine Drivers.

The people who survive will be trained and specialized and experienced enough to be architects, directing AI work, and QAs, checking AI work before it "goes live." People will gain experience by being given "architecture/QA" positions over elements that are small and insignificant. 

Eg: "This job will focus on the quality and design of tasks that AI agents use to execute lawn mowing functions."

Originally written 10 September 2026. Minor updates made periodically.

- Ai is causing programming to change software engineering. These changes will prefigure the changes coming in other fields. The longer we do software engineering, the more engineers spend time thinking about high level abstractions, not considering the detailed high/low voltages (1s, 0s). I no longer worry about what a .find() does under the hood, at least most of the time, I simply know it gets me a matching result. Similarly, developers are shifting away from writing each line of code, and towards reviewing AI generated code. This pattern will continue until engineers are just reviewing interfaces/contracts. We won't care what the details are doing most of the time, just like we don't care what the .find() does most of the time. I don't think this will eliminate the need for developers who know how things work all the way down the stack, but it is already reducing time spent in the weeds. Big picture thinking, strategy, communication, and architecture will become more valuable.
